Certainly! The `\rowcolor` command in LaTeX is used to set the background color of a row in a table. This command is provided by the `colortbl` or `xcolor` package (with the `table` option).

Here’s **how to use `\rowcolor`**:

1. **Include the package**:
   ```latex
   \usepackage[table]{xcolor}
   ```
   or
   ```latex
   \usepackage{colortbl}
   ```

2. **Use `\rowcolor` within a table**:

   ```latex
   \begin{tabular}{ |c|c|c| }
   \hline
   \rowcolor{gray!30}
   Header1 & Header2 & Header3 \\
   \hline
   Data1 & Data2 & Data3 \\
   \rowcolor{yellow}
   Data4 & Data5 & Data6 \\
   \hline
   \end{tabular}
   ```

**Useful notes:**
- `\rowcolor{colorname}` must be placed before the row you want to color.
- Common color names include `red`, `green`, `blue`, `yellow`, `gray`, or custom colors via `\definecolor`.
- Use opacity with (for instance) `gray!30` for 30% gray.

---

**Full Example:**

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[table]{xcolor}

\begin{document}

\begin{tabular}{ |c|c|c| }
\hline
\rowcolor{gray!30}
Header1 & Header2 & Header3 \\
\hline
Data1 & Data2 & Data3 \\
\rowcolor{yellow}
Data4 & Data5 & Data6 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

\end{document}
```

